<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Niklotings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Mecklenburg, also called the Niklotings, was a dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>, a prince of the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> who died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in 1160. His son Pribislav accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, and the family ruled Mecklenburg as lords, as dukes from 1348 and as grand dukes from 1815. Its lines in Mecklenburg-Schwerin and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> reigned until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="House of Mecklenburg" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glasshouse, using elements by Sodacan, Trondivers · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Mecklenburg_-_Schwerin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Mecklenburg was a North German family of Polabian origin and one of the longest-ruling families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was established by Pribislav, an Obotrite prince who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and accepted the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, his fallen father's enemy, becoming lord of Mecklenburg. The name derives from Mikla Burg, 'big castle', the family's main fortress. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> were subsequently Germanized.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the fourteenth century the dukes pursued a claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> through descent from Scandinavian noblewomen, among them Christina, a daughter of King Sverker II. Albert II, Duke of Mecklenburg, married Euphemia of Sweden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and their second son, Albert III, deposed his uncle and became king of Sweden. The family held Sweden from 1364 to 1389. They also claimed Norway through Euphemia, a granddaughter of Haakon V, but when Olav IV died in 1387 the regent Margaret chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, whose mother Maria of Mecklenburg was Euphemia's eldest granddaughter.</p><p>In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries the duchy was divided several times among agnates, into principalities such as Mecklenburg-Schwerin, Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. By the late eighteenth century most parts had returned to Schwerin, and the land stayed divided between Schwerin and Strelitz until the end of the monarchy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> recognised both states as grand duchies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The main line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> died out in 1918, and the Grand Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin stepped in as regent, but questions of succession involving a junior Strelitz branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> were unsettled when both monarchies became republics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-the-netherlands">Queen Juliana</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, who reigned from 1948 to 1980, was an agnatic member of the house. The Schwerin line became extinct in the male line on 31 July 2001, which left Strelitz as the surviving male line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Mecklenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Niklotings</b>, descendants of the Obotrite prince Niklot, had ruled the Lordship of Mecklenburg since the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After the extinction of the Güstrow line in 1695, a succession dispute in the <b>House of Mecklenburg</b> was settled by the Treaty of Hamburg in 1701.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a></li></ul></section>
